 in the country there's no  public defender's office and so when  there's a crime especially a serious  crime  the local judges have to assign the  defense to the local bar  and i experienced it when there's a  really terrible crime the lawyers really  don't want the cases  because they're you know they're very  difficult this is a story about a  you know a dead cop in a small town and  no lawyer wants to step  in for the defense jake sure didn't want  to but he was kind of forced to and he  gradually uh realized that it was him or  no one so he  came to the defense of this kid and the  kid's family and  and we go to trial you uh john obviously  this is a this is a very  contentious time in terms of the police  and the justice system  did that play into your thinking in  writing this book and has your own  attitude toward the justice system  changed in the years that you've been  writing these books  it certainly has changed it goes back to  about 15 years ago when i wrote a book  called the innocent man a non-fiction  account of a wrongful conviction and  i think from that point on i became more  aware of all the problems we  have with the criminal justice system a  lot of issues  i've written about a lot of them and i  will continue to do so because that's  where my  interests lie uh but no when i started  the book i was not really thinking about  uh the police or those issues it just  kind of happened  and there's no real mystery here in the  first chapter you know who killed who  and so the question becomes how do you  handle a 16 year old kid charged with  capital murder  the story takes place in 1990 and back  then it was common in the death belt to  put  teenagers on trial for capital murder  and so the book sort of explores  you know the issues there what are how  do you handle these kids  youngsters who commit terrible crimes  and and how do you  protect the victims and i don't really  answer the question in the book because  i don't know the answer  yeah j